-- title:
--   Invertibility of an invertible ideal sheaf pulled back to a reduced scheme
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes (in a fixed universe) with $X$ reduced, let $I$ be an ideal sheaf datum on $Y$, and let $g \colon X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes. Assume $I$ satisfies `IsInvertible`: for every point $y$ of $Y$ there are an affine open $U \subseteq Y$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(Y,U)$ with $y \in Y_{\mathrm{basicOpen}}(f)$, together with an element $a$ of the monoid of non-zero-divisors of $\Gamma(Y, \mathrm{affineBasicOpen}(f))$ such that the ideal assigned by $I$ to the affine open $\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}(f)$ is the principal ideal $(a)$. Assume further that every point $\xi$ of $X$ admitting no proper generisation, i.e. such that every $y \in X$ with $y \rightsquigarrow \xi$ satisfies $y = \xi$, lies outside the support of the comap ideal sheaf $I.\mathrm{comap}\ g$ (the ideal sheaf on $X$ generated by the pullback of $I$). Then $I.\mathrm{comap}\ g$ is itself invertible in the same sense: every point of $X$ has an affine neighbourhood of the above shape on which this ideal sheaf is generated by a single non-zero-divisor.
--
--   This is the statement that an effective Cartier divisor on $Y$ restricts to an effective Cartier divisor along $g$ whenever $X$ is reduced and no generic point of an irreducible component of $X$ lies in the inverse-image support; it generalises the familiar case of an integral source, where the condition reads that the pullback support is not all of $X$. It is used to restrict a divisor with no component supported on a fibre to a reduced, reducible special fibre in the study of models of the modular curve $X_1(p)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_IsInvertible_comap_of_isReduced_of_forall_specializes.lean

